{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>After I have grown old, shall I have pleasure?</b> One must examine why Sarah laughed, for she saw that a miracle had been performed for her contrary to nature and she had returned to the days of her youth, and she should have understood that this miracle was not performed for her in vain. All the more so Abraham, for the matter is not impossible — a very old man can still beget children. And if she perceived in him great weakness, so that she thought that procreation was impossible from his side — if so, why did the Holy One, blessed be He, alter it for the sake of peace? For why should it be evil in Abraham's eyes that she spoke the truth? And the language of 'shall I indeed bear' is unclear according to Rashi's interpretation." "And it is nearly to be heard in this matter, that Sarah did not doubt the power of El, blessed be He, and laughed at the promise — 'your reward shall be very great' (Genesis 15:1) — for she thought that since they are near the gates of death and will not remember many days of their lives, surely by the way of nature they will not merit to raise the son and marry him off to a woman, and this is not a complete promise, for they will not rejoice in him much. Therefore she laughed and said: granted, as for me — since a miracle was performed for me, that 'after I have grown old, I have had pleasure' (Genesis 18:12), surely my own merit caused this for me, and this merit will stand for me also to live many days. But 'my lord is old' (Genesis 18:12) and has not returned to the days of his youth, for I feel in him no addition of strength, and if so, surely he has no great merit, for the victory over the kings has already consumed him, and he will not live long contrary to nature — and if so, why does he need a son? And HaShem said, 'Why did Sarah laugh, saying, shall I indeed bear?' (Genesis 18:13) — if it is true that I shall indeed bear, nevertheless what of it? Behold, 'I have grown old' (Genesis 18:13), for I have no merit that would add to my days. And in this the Holy One, blessed be He, altered it for the sake of peace, for she was attributing the paucity of merit to him and not to herself. 'Is anything too wondrous for HaShem?' (Genesis 18:14) — for I know the number of their days, that they will merit to raise him. Another matter: since it says 'and Sarah laughed within herself' (Genesis 18:12), this implies she laughed in thought, for out of fear she did not bring out to her lips all the laughter that was in her heart. Therefore He said, 'Is anything too wondrous for HaShem?' — do I not know what is hidden in her heart?"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:42.630496564Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}